  "name": "POTASSIUM (K+)",
  "code": "K+",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<ul><li style=\"font-size: 10px;\">Normal range for serum potassium is typically between 3.5 to 5.0 millimoles per liter (mmol/L). However, reference ranges may vary slightly depending on the laboratory that performs the test.</li><li style=\"font-size: 10px;\">If the serum potassium level is within the normal range, it typically indicates that the body's potassium levels are balanced, and the organs and tissues are functioning normally.</li><li style=\"font-size: 10px;\">If the serum potassium level is above the normal range, it may indicate hyperkalemia, which can be caused by kidney disease, dehydration, certain medications, or other factors. Hyperkalemia can be a serious medical condition and requires prompt medical attention.</li><li style=\"font-size: 10px;\">If the serum potassium level is below the normal range, it may indicate hypokalemia, which can be caused by a variety of factors, including kidney disease, diarrhea, vomiting, or certain medications. Hypokalemia can also be a serious medical condition and requires prompt medical attention.</li></ul>",
